skill: Advanced Java

@ JDBC | B
Which JDBC interface should you use to run a parameterized query and help prevent SQL injection?
= PreparedStatement
- Statement
- ResultSet
- DriverManager
? PreparedStatement sends parameters separately from the SQL text, so user input is never executed as SQL.

@ JDBC | B
Which JDBC type represents the rows returned by a SELECT query?
= ResultSet
- Connection
- Driver
- Savepoint
? A ResultSet is a cursor over the rows produced by executing a query.

@ JDBC | B
What does ResultSet.next() return when there are no more rows?
= false
- true
- null
- 0
? next() moves the cursor forward and returns false once it passes the last row.

@ JDBC | I
Which JDBC method returns the number of rows affected by an INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE?
= executeUpdate
- executeQuery
- getResultSet
- next
? executeUpdate returns an int row count for DML statements. executeQuery is meant for SELECT and returns a ResultSet.

@ JDBC | I
What is the recommended way to make sure JDBC resources are closed?
= Use try-with-resources
- Rely on garbage collection to close them
- Close only the Connection and ignore the rest
- Call System.gc() after the query
? Connection, Statement and ResultSet implement AutoCloseable, so try-with-resources closes them automatically even when an exception occurs.

@ JDBC | I
What does calling connection.setAutoCommit(false) do?
= Starts manual transaction control, so changes are saved only after commit()
- Disables the connection permanently
- Commits automatically after every statement
- Makes the connection read-only
? With auto-commit off you group statements into a transaction and decide when to commit or roll back.

@ JDBC | B
Which JDBC URL has the correct format for MySQL?
= jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb
- mysql:jdbc://localhost/mydb
- http://localhost:3306/mydb
- jdbc://mysql:localhost:3306/mydb
? A JDBC URL starts with jdbc:, then the sub-protocol (mysql), then //host:port/database.

@ Multithreading | B
Which method starts a new thread of execution?
= start()
- run()
- begin()
- execute()
? start() asks the JVM to create a new thread, which then calls run().

@ Multithreading | I
What happens when you call run() directly on a Thread object instead of start()?
= The code runs in the calling thread and no new thread is created
- A new thread is created and runs the code
- An IllegalThreadStateException is always thrown
- It does not compile
? run() is an ordinary method call. Only start() creates a new thread.

@ Multithreading | I
What does the synchronized keyword provide?
= Mutual exclusion, so only one thread at a time can run the guarded code for a given lock
- Faster execution of the method
- Automatic creation of a thread pool
- Permission to catch checked exceptions
? synchronized acquires the object's monitor lock so competing threads must wait, which prevents race conditions.

@ Multithreading | A
What is a deadlock?
= Two or more threads wait forever for locks held by each other
- A thread finishes before the others
- A thread runs with the lowest priority
- A thread is terminated by an exception
? In a deadlock each thread holds a lock the other needs, so none of them can proceed.

@ Servlets and JSP | B
Which HttpServlet method handles HTTP GET requests?
= doGet
- onGet
- handleGet
- readRequest
? HttpServlet dispatches GET requests to doGet(HttpServletRequest, HttpServletResponse).

@ Servlets and JSP | I
Which servlet lifecycle method is called once when the servlet is first initialized?
= init()
- service()
- destroy()
- doGet()
? The container calls init() once, then service() for each request, and destroy() when the servlet is removed.

@ Servlets and JSP | I
Which JSP element prints the value of an expression into the response?
= <%= expression %>
- <% code %>
- <%! declaration %>
- <%@ directive %>
? The expression tag <%= ... %> evaluates the expression and writes the result to the output.
